ations done on  the file and then after doing the  functions for this piece we had to  generalize the code to be more  applicable to other songs as well so as  you can see here is the same data  generator however if we play the first  two seconds of this piece on the perfect  side and this was the perfect for if you  know the song entertainer and if we  played the right side now  [Music]  so as you can tell there's a big change  between the two so it just the center  general away with entertainer and then  we later after this we had to think  about real-world applicability so what  we did was we imported in an actual MIDI  file into this program and we thought  does our programs don't work  successfully it did and then yeah and  then now we have the visualization so  how we did this is we took the data  generated from the from our program that  looked at the mistakes that were made so  for the timing function if you can see  on the x-axis it's the time interval  between the songs so from zero to two  seconds that's the first quadrant from  two to four seconds it's the second  quadrant and then we have the cumulative  error on the side and it's just counting  how many times you make an error during  that certain time instants and then with  the with the timing error what the  timing graph does it also allows for  students and teachers to see during what  time interval do you see the most  mountainous takes whether it be from  properties of the song or from a  player's just disposition on the  difficulties of playing it and then on  the note graph you see on the x-axis we  have their practice MIDI notes and then  under y-axis we have the perfect MIDI  notes so what you would what this graph  would show is whether a student played a  B instead of an A and then what it would  do was would point it would it will  place a point on the XY axis and then  depending on how many mistakes you make  during that song it would go up by one  two or three or how many times you'll  make a mistake so in general if you had  a perfect piece with no makes mistakes  in the note it would just be a flat  plane okay so we'd like to see our  project expand in several directions in  the future so currently as you see it's  capable with the short term analysis but  we'd also like to extend it to long term  analysis because in the real world  teachers would probably want to see  trends in improvement along with the  current playing ability and we've  started implementing lesson number and  improvement factor variables to find  patterns of recurring mistakes we could  also analyze more complex technique  signatures so ad  these on top of the skill sets that we  already have could enhance the spectrum  of skill level analysis and provide  better feedback for a wider range of  songs and techniques so for example when  one kind of technique signature would be  syncopation and syncopation is when you  intentionally deviate from the normal  flow of rhythm so this could be another  factor that would be taken into account  when we analyze a student's recordings  our user interface is currently very  basic and the only way to import certain  files is to of course call import and  then specify the file location but this  might be a little bit cumbersome and  difficult for piano teachers and  students especially little kids so we'd  like to implement a more polish  interface that also generates our output  in a much cleaner fashion so to recap  this project was just one example of an  attempt to grow express and assess  student understanding in the piano  skills context so all of us are pianists  and we all had this was a useful  background to draw on when developing  this project but in the future projects  like this could be expanded to all kinds  of skill sets in order to capture  behavioral data which can then be  translated into inferential data  ultimately allowing us to predict and  improve future performance thank you for  listening  [Applause]
 

